---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/for/supplements';
const title = 'Choosing Shopify SMS apps for supplement stores';
const desc = 'Evaluate claims review, subscription exclusions and replenishment data before choosing a supplement SMS workflow. A reorder reminder should follow an actual purchase context, not an inferred health condition.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">SMS operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">

<h2>Start with the product and the message</h2>
<p>A supplement catalog can contain very different ingredients and claims. Give the prospective sender the actual SKU list, destination countries, sample message and landing page before committing to acquisition spend. Ask for written category eligibility and registration requirements. An app accepting a Shopify connection does not establish that its messaging suppliers accept every product you sell.</p>
<p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.fda.gov/food/dietary-supplements-guidance-documents-regulatory-information/dietary-supplement-labeling-guide-chapter-vi-claims">FDA claims guide</a> distinguishes health, nutrient-content and structure/function claims. These categories have different conditions; a marketing text should not improvise a disease-treatment promise from a product description. The <a class="link" href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/health-products-compliance-guidance">FTC health-products guidance</a> addresses both express and implied advertising claims and supporting scientific evidence. Review the text, image and linked destination together. A disclaimer is not a substitute for substantiating the message a customer reasonably takes away.</p>
<h2>Keep a claims approval record</h2>
<p>For each campaign, record the SKU, exact proposed wording, linked page version, supporting evidence reference, reviewer and approval date. Freeze that version for scheduling. If someone changes the benefit claim, testimonial, image or destination after review, return the campaign to the reviewer. This is a proposed operating process, not a claim that any named app supplies regulatory approval.</p>
<p>A neutral reorder link can still lead to an unreviewed sales page. Include the landing page in review and keep health advice out of the marketing reply inbox. Route questions about symptoms, interactions or adverse experiences to the appropriate support process rather than making individualized product recommendations through an automation.</p>
<h2>Build replenishment around fulfillment and subscription state</h2>
<p>Map each reminder to the item actually fulfilled, pack quantity and order status. Order date alone can overstate consumption after a shipping delay. Do not infer a medical need from purchase history or claim that the customer is running low with certainty. Let the customer manage reminder preferences and use neutral language about viewing their reorder options.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Customer state</th><th>Proposed treatment</th><th>Data needed before queueing</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Active recurring subscription</td><td>Suppress a one-time reorder promotion that duplicates the next shipment</td><td>Current subscription status, next charge and skip/cancel events</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gift order</td><td>Exclude from personal consumption reminders unless the buyer separately requests them</td><td>Explicit gift marker or preference; do not guess from address alone</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refund, cancellation or open complaint</td><td>Stop the sales sequence and let support resolve the issue</td><td>Refund and support events available to the send decision</td></tr>
<tr><td>One-time repeat buyer</td><td>Offer a reorder route only while eligible and opted in</td><td>Fulfilled SKU, intervening orders, stock and current SMS permission</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Recheck those conditions at send time. A subscription cancellation may arrive after an audience was exported; a stale segment should not override the latest preference. If subscription data is unavailable, hold that automation rather than presenting its exclusions as working.</p>
<h2>Use a demonstration brief instead of a ranking</h2>
<p>Ask each shortlisted app to demonstrate one purchase, a delayed fulfillment, a subscription skip, a refund and a later opt-out. These are proposed acceptance cases, not tests performed for this guide. Require an inspectable reason when a message is skipped and identify the team member responsible for missing events. Compare setup effort, event delay, support ownership and total messaging cost on the same workload.</p>
<p>Catalog entry points include <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a>; these links are not a category approval or performance order. <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy currently advertises an SMS/MMS beta</a> on paid plans using a US/Canada toll-free number. For a supplement use case, confirm category approval, subscription-event coverage and beta operating limits directly rather than assuming either email-only scope or Shopify workflow parity.</p>
<h2>Coordinate education without creating a second sales trigger</h2>
<p>Email can carry longer product instructions and preference management; it needs the same claims review as SMS. Give one system ownership of a replenishment attempt. Record the order or subscription event that closes the attempt, and cancel pending sends in both tools. An unopened email is not proof that an additional text is welcome.</p>
<p>Separate service notices from promotional reorders in both consent records and templates. Review local requirements before adding an offer to a shipment or account notification. See the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent workflow</a> for capture and revocation records.</p>
<h2>Measure repeat contribution, not replenishment attribution alone</h2>
<p>Budget platform charges, message segments, carrier charges where applicable, discounts, fulfillment and returns. Separate one-time repurchases from scheduled subscription orders that would occur without the reminder. Compare eligible randomized groups over a complete reorder window before interpreting a revenue difference as incremental. Do not promise a universal reminder interval or weekly cap for all supplements.</p>
<p>Before expanding, hand the owner a claims approval archive, subscription suppression map, unresolved-event report and contribution worksheet. Pause a flow when a SKU or claim changes until its review is complete. This guide supplies selection and operating criteria; product eligibility and jurisdiction-specific legal review remain merchant-specific decisions.</p>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
